, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80910" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d98c299ad4517f10439ec04ee71f463cfdfdb79775a78be8d8d705fbf83dbb73"} {"question" "q-80910" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80910" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] ["math" "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] ["math" "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t/2}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t/2},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] ["math" "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] ["math" "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t/2}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t/2},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80910" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-326236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-326236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1-(-1) & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1-(-1)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{3},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-326236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d98c299ad4517f10439ec04ee71f463cfdfdb79775a78be8d8d705fbf83dbb73"} {"question" "q-326236" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-326236" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-326236" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =-1.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1+1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3+1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{w}_{1}+4{w}_{2}=-2 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1-2{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=-2,b=-1,$ and $a+b=-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d98c299ad4517f10439ec04ee71f463cfdfdb79775a78be8d8d705fbf83dbb73"} {"question" "q-80604" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80604" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80604" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80915" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80915" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3+3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3+3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+5{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{5},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{5}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80915" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d98c299ad4517f10439ec04ee71f463cfdfdb79775a78be8d8d705fbf83dbb73"} {"question" "q-80915" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80915" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80915" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-325120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-325120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2-2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ 1{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=1,$ and $a+b=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-325120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d98c299ad4517f10439ec04ee71f463cfdfdb79775a78be8d8d705fbf83dbb73"} {"question" "q-325120" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-325120" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-325120" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =-2.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1+2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3+2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}-{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ {w}_{1}-{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is the same as the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}={w}_{2}+1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get ${w}_{1}=1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=1,b=1,$ and $a+b=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d98c299ad4517f10439ec04ee71f463cfdfdb79775a78be8d8d705fbf83dbb73"} {"question" "q-80603" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80603" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80603" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2+2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2+2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{5} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{5} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}. \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2+5t){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d98c299ad4517f10439ec04ee71f463cfdfdb79775a78be8d8d705fbf83dbb73"} {"question" "q-80927" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80927" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2+5t){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}(1-t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2+5t){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80927" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2+5t){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80928" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =-1.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80928" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-7+1 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 5+1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6{w}_{1}-4{w}_{2}=-2 \\\\ 9{w}_{1}+6{w}_{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3},$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+5([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1-10t){e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+15t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80928" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d98c299ad4517f10439ec04ee71f463cfdfdb79775a78be8d8d705fbf83dbb73"} {"question" "q-80928" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80928" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}(1-10t){e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+15t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}(1-2t){e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+3t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}(1-2t){e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+15t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80928" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}(1-10t){e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+15t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-326238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-326238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3-(-3) & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3-(-3)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}-6{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{{c}_{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{{c}_{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1-12t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-326238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d98c299ad4517f10439ec04ee71f463cfdfdb79775a78be8d8d705fbf83dbb73"} {"question" "q-326238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-326238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}(1+t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ (2-12t){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}(1-12t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ (2-12t){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}(1-12t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (2-12t){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-326238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}(1-12t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80932" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =7.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80932" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =7$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}10-7 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4-7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{w}_{1}+{w}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -9{w}_{1}-3{w}_{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{{w}_{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3},$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{7t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{7t}+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{7t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{7t}-3[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{7t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -9t{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80932" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d98c299ad4517f10439ec04ee71f463cfdfdb79775a78be8d8d705fbf83dbb73"} {"question" "q-80932" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80932" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -3t{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -9t{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -t{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{7t} \\\\ -3t{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{7t} \\\\ -9t{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-80932" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -9t{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-325135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/state.json" "file_sha256" "e37f70755b185d2ae67cef804224ee1a7afdb2dc832c333a6720207626d86172"} {"question" "q-325135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4-4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4-4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+2{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{{c}_{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{{c}_{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(1-t){e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers