 solution to the matrix differential equation ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/31069ee6b2fce087880c8d94ec10bbb724e5f1d07be9adc6040c0a6b3aa2ff8a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/example-7048.json" "file_sha256" "36f6f470587611aeffc403bec5d81b7d9c20bd3a5a1caf8b065064ca3df60af4"} {"question" "e-7048" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/8103dd1abfd1ba9488509a0e858113e05e877956d99e6b2d1eb1ac2bd6b85c7e.png)\nthe formula for the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/6425989fee48f2b78a4f2c8e1cbda9c0f64597600d2b0a054f9dea020d4e0f81.png)\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/c20e24c2457c66423fa2ff066a6d34dc1d5e2e044703236310f1a3f1aa48ce65.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/example-7048.json" "file_sha256" "36f6f470587611aeffc403bec5d81b7d9c20bd3a5a1caf8b065064ca3df60af4"} {"question" "e-7049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/1ead7e2a9fff08d6ec1d8e70c3f9f0c10729a4899227c53eabe7a5212feb1659.png)\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/36b5e6757a7849e9e405883da345dadfbcc3130b517d05e117f4fb6fb4338ae8.png)\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/example-7049.json" "file_sha256" "e7ba4a835934c5a00456437b827b121b6dfed69b40653f1ac4b4cc7f517ee996"} {"question" "e-7049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/f9f940992f15a14e58f7eee0b8171e8e0e437787d6340618fcd1f23b84d333a8.png)\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda=-4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/9a7426e0e17bb57e1a8bfb24dca5ca3a579809708af3055184f9cfc2728957bc.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/f3e3bae74afa48fd689bb48dc317af52e44914f9f35d32375463d3705268b377.png)\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{w},$ we solve the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{v}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-4+4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4+4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/6e5c9442761d05d62ccc3ce56cbe09794e57b931987e3e4ef33d93d7cfb1e39f.png)\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\nw_2 = 1.\n$$\n\nThe variable $w_1$ is a free variable. Setting $w_1 = 0,$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/6b54faff854b99c0c86b3de477cc2dde8b5a714bfc296c59ed6757f9bc250f77.png)\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/96ee1e3a1af5c670b87cd06e406ac16b0fa9f5e5305b2fe95dc395c079d291da.png)\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0, b=1,$ and $a+b=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/example-7049.json" "file_sha256" "e7ba4a835934c5a00456437b827b121b6dfed69b40653f1ac4b4cc7f517ee996"} {"question" "e-7050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/9459f772e062490c525f6c7bc083c02d0ed5dc3bc4c331f642653aa4f9bf5515.png)\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda=3.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/example-7050.json" "file_sha256" "8aa5655d63fe3188ddf975f0127d05e07d5e9a01f4c011521c38313f3c2d755b"} {"question" "e-7050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/df4b98b4799865dbf40e765bda0fa6176157139d99a62bd1febe48d7c4600c8c.png)\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda=3$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/8032d75d584838d0743f2e9217c25844af637d07261dd2d6a89add9df531fdb1.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/aa5146c331315952e36051be1126010bbcaf706f2cbc1c886c56b0e92b468fa4.png)\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1  \\mathbf{v}e^{\\lambda t} + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}t e^{\\lambda t} +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{w},$ we solve the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{v}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1-3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 7-3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/b0d075668031a0decad6d092f2f03c3f74eacdcff0546e99e8373901c5a910ac.png)\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\nw_1 =\\dfrac{1}{2} - 2 w_2 .\n$$\n\nThe variable $w_2$ is a free variable. Setting $w_2 = 0,$  we get $w_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2},$ and therefore\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/726b5737809539337c1a8a259727a47b9bdacdf81de92a4a35783518df9dcaa0.png)\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/a1a3e46d8352ceee549fb5040261d8edf036d8fb32fc5fc5b960cab07097970f.png)\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/assets/2e3a20e62437546fb2498d1575c9ed2cd268c43b968174ea7e0139977fb6fbd5.png) Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-2([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(4t-3){e}^{3t} \\\\ (1-2t){e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067736/example-7050.json" "file_sha256" "8aa5655d63fe3188ddf975f0127d05e07d5e9a01f4c011521c38313f3c2d755b"} {"question" "q-80548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligne