}te^{2t} + \\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{2t}), c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R$,\n\nwhat is the value of $a + b$?"] [17592186157678 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t), x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186157006 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186157006 :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ x_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nthe formula for the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled.\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186157007 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b$?"] [17592186157007 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda=-4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we get an eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t} + c_2 (\\mathbf{v} te^{\\lambda t} + \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\,)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{w}$, we solve the equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(A - λI)w &= v \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 + 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 + 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}w_{1} \\\\ w_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}w_{1} \\\\ w_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n0w_{1} + 1w_{2} = 1; 0w_{1} + 0w_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n$w_2 = 1$.\n\nThe variable $w_1$ is a free variable. Setting $w_1 = 0$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0, b=1$, and\n\n$$\na+b=1\n$$"] [17592186157008 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda=3$.*"] [17592186157008 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda=3$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we get an eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1 \\mathbf{v}e^{\\lambda t} + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}t e^{\\lambda t} + \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t})\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{w}$, we solve the equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(A - λI)w &= v \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 - 3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 7 - 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}w_{1} \\\\ w_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}w_{1} \\\\ w_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n-4w_{1} - 8w_{2} =-2; 2w_{1} + 4w_{2} = 1\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\nw_1 =\\dfrac{1}{2} - 2 w_2\n$$\n\nThe variable $w_2$ is a free variable. Setting $w_2 = 0$, we get\n\n$$\nw_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2c_{1} + \\frac{1}{2}c_{2} \\\\ c_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2c_{1} + \\frac{1}{2}c_{2} \\\\ c_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} - 2(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}te^{3t} + \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}te^{3t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}(4t - 3)e^{3t} \\\\ (1 - 2t)e^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186155226 17592186155227 17592186155228 17592186155229 17592186155230 17592186155238 17592186155239 17592186155240 17592186155241 17592186155242 17592186155585 17592186155586 17592186